<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Silesian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1744">1744</a>–<a href="/years/1745">1745</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Silesian War (1744–1745) was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a>. Fearing Austria would retake Silesia, Frederick II invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in 1744 and then won the battles of Hohenfriedberg, Soor and Kesselsdorf in 1745. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> of December 1745 confirmed Prussian possession of Silesia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-silesian-war.jpg" alt="Second Silesian War" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Röchling · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hohenfriedeberg_-_Attack_of_Prussian_Infantry_-_1745.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-berlin-1742">Treaty of Berlin</a> of July 1742 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> ceded most of Silesia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in return for its neutrality, and then recovered its losses against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In September 1743 Austria, Britain-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> and Savoy-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> allied under the Treaty of Worms, which made no mention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s earlier guarantee of Prussian Silesia. Fearing an anti-Prussian coalition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> formed the League of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> with Bavaria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> and the Electoral Palatinate on 22 May 1744, ostensibly to restore the territories of Emperor Charles VII, and concluded a treaty with France on 5 June. On 7 August Prussia declared its intervention.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>About 70,000 Prussians entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in three columns on 15 August 1744, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> surrendered on 16 September. The French failed to hinder the return of Prince Charles Alexander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>'s army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, and Otto Ferdinand von Traun avoided battle while harassing Prussian supply lines, so the Prussians abandoned Bohemia in November. In January 1745 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, Britain-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> formed a Quadruple Alliance, and Charles VII died on 20 January. After the Battle of Pfaffenhofen on 15 April, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> made peace by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fussen">Füssen</a>.</p><p>Frederick routed the Austrian-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> army at Hohenfriedberg on 4 June, and on 29 September he held off a surprise attack at Soor. Francis Stephen of Lorraine was elected emperor as Francis I on 13 September. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> struck toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, Frederick scattered Prince Charles's army at Hennersdorf on 23 November, Leopold I of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> destroyed Frederick Augustus Rutowsky's Saxons at Kesselsdorf on 15 December, and the Prussians occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> on 18 December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> of 25 December 1745 confirmed Prussian Silesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klodzko">Glatz</a>; Frederick recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-holy-roman-emperor">Francis I</a> as emperor and accepted the Pragmatic Sanction, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> paid one million rixdollars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>. The victories of 1745 won Frederick acclaim as a commander, and he began to be called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>. The war marked the start of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s rise toward great-power status and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and Prussia lasting enemies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> refused to sign the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle in 1748 because it guaranteed Prussian Silesia, and she reformed her army and diplomacy to recover it, leading to the Third Silesian War in 1756.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Silesian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Second Silesian War</b> ended in 1745 with the Treaty of Dresden, which confirmed Prussian possession of Silesia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-7">7 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Second Silesian War</b>, part of the War of the Austrian Succession, Frederick the Great's Prussian army decisively defeated an Austro-Saxon army under Prince Charles Alexander of Lorraine.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-4">4 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
